************************** Routing ***************************
1. Conventional routing:- mvc defined URL routing pattern
2. Attribute routing:- attribute routing decorated over controller or action name.

1.Conventional routing
configure routing for UseMvc() middleware
		app.UseMvc(routes=>{
			// 1st param- route name
			//2nd param- route pattern. 'home' is a static part of route url
			//id param is optional
			//= used to give default route
			routes.MapRoute("1stroute","home/{controller= Home}/{action= Index}/{id?}");  
		});
		
2. Attribute routing:-
	[Route("")]	// empty URL will be navigated here
	[Route("Home")] //if URL has only controller defined , it will point to Index action
 	[Route("Home/Index")]   // with compelte URL route mapping
	[Route("Home/Index/{id?}")] //optional id param
	public ViewResult Index(string id){
	}
	
	[Route("Home")]
	public class HomeController: Controller{
		
		//"~" in routing will ignore controller attribute routing
		// localhost:2020 will point to this action
		[Route("~/")]
		public ViewResult Index(){}

		//localhost:2020/Home/Details will point to this action method
		[Route("Details")]
		public ViewResult Details(){}
	}
	
token based Attribute routing:- it aumatically replaces actual controller/action name in pace in routing URL
		It's a dynamic way of defining controller/ action name, so that in future if action/ controller name is being changed, we dont need to change routing attribute. 
	[Route("[controller]")]
	public class XyzController: Controller{			

		//localhost:2020/Home/Details will point to this action method
		[Route("[action]")]
		public ViewResult Details(){}
	}

